c11: TIME_MONOTONIC is CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW on Darwin (the kernel's DRM deadline clock)

Mesa computes absolute DRM deadlines (DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_WAIT, timeline waits) from
os_time_get_nano() = timespec_get(TIME_MONOTONIC). The kernel's DRM layer (LinuxKPI ktime_get)
counts mach_absolute_time, i.e. CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW. On the M1 Darwin's CLOCK_MONOTONIC was 10.06 s
behind it (m1-vulkan/test/wait-probe: a 300 ms wait took 0.0 ms with CLOCK_MONOTONIC, 302 ms with
CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW), so every finite vkWaitForFences returned VK_TIMEOUT at once.
cnd_monotonic.c converts its deadlines with the same timespec_get, so it stays consistent.
Upstream: no (a SiliconForest kernel property).

--- a/src/c11/impl/time.c
+++ b/src/c11/impl/time.c
@@ -106,7 +106,16 @@
         break;
 #ifdef CLOCK_MONOTONIC
     case TIME_MONOTONIC:
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW)
+        /* SiliconForest: the kernel's DRM layer compares absolute deadlines
+         * (DRM_IOCTL_SYNCOBJ_WAIT and friends) with its uptime, which is
+         * CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW (mach_absolute_time); Darwin's CLOCK_MONOTONIC is
+         * another clock (about 10 s behind it on the M1), and every finite wait
+         * returned at once. Mesa's monotonic time is the kernel's. */
+        if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW, ts) == 0)
+#else
         if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, ts) == 0)
+#endif
             return base;
         break;
 #endif
